<h2>Two doctrines, one gate question</h2>
<p>The first question in every impossibility dispute is whether the contract speaks. A force majeure clause is the parties&rsquo; own allocation of supervening risk: it defines qualifying events, the consequence (suspension, extension of time, renegotiation, termination after a defined period), and the procedure for invoking it. Because the parties contemplated the contingency, the court&rsquo;s task is interpretation, not rescue — Section 32 treats the contract as contingent on the defined event, and relief is exactly what the clause gives, no more. Section 56&rsquo;s second paragraph is the residual rule: a contract to do an act which, after the contract is made, becomes impossible or unlawful, becomes void when the act becomes impossible or unlawful. It is not a sympathy jurisdiction. The event must strike at the root of the contract, destroying its very foundation, and it must be one the parties did not provide for and could not have averted.</p>

<h2>What qualifies — and what does not</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>Typically outside the doctrines</h3><p>Price escalation and input shortages that make performance costlier; a party&rsquo;s own financial difficulties; delays caused by the invoking party; events foreseeable at signing and silently accepted; difficulties that an alternative mode of performance could overcome.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>Potentially within them</h3><p>Destruction of the specific subject matter; supervening illegality — a statutory ban, a licence lawfully revoked; government orders that prohibit (not merely complicate) the contracted activity; events named in the clause, if their causal effect on performance is proved.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The burden throughout is on the party claiming excuse. It must prove the event, prove the causal chain from event to non-performance, and prove that performance — not profitability — was hit. Where only part of the obligation was affected, or performance remained possible by a different route, courts confine relief accordingly. And a party already in breach when the event struck cannot ordinarily shelter behind it: force majeure excuses the diligent, not the defaulting.</p>

<h2>The anatomy of a working force majeure clause</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Definition.</strong> Named events (fire, flood, epidemic, war, embargo, government prohibition) plus a controlled residuary phrase — with the consequence that unlisted commercial shocks stay outside.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Causation threshold.</strong> &ldquo;Prevented&rdquo; is a harder test than &ldquo;hindered or delayed&rdquo;; the drafting choice decides how much disruption triggers the clause.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Notice machinery.</strong> Time-bound written notice with particulars, updates while the event continues, and a duty to mitigate — the compliance record later becomes the evidence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Consequences ladder.</strong> Suspension of affected obligations first, extension of time next, and a termination right for either party if the event outlasts a defined period — with accounting for amounts already paid.</div>
</div>

<h2>Frustration&rsquo;s all-or-nothing consequence</h2>
<p>Section 56 does not suspend a contract or rewrite it; it avoids the contract as a whole from the moment performance became impossible. That bluntness has two practical edges. First, a party who wants the relationship to survive a temporary shock is better served by a clause-based suspension than by pleading frustration, which kills the contract it invokes. Second, once a contract is void, Section 65 restitution follows: advantages received under it — advances, deposits, part payments for which no counter-performance moved — must be restored. The same section of the Act also makes a promisor who knew of the impossibility while the promisee did not liable to compensate for the loss the promisee suffers through non-performance.</p>

<h2>Litigating the shock event in Delhi</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plead the route.</strong> A written statement that mixes &ldquo;force majeure under the agreement&rdquo; with &ldquo;frustration under Section 56&rdquo; without distinguishing them invites the reply that the clause occupies the field. Elect, or plead in the alternative with discipline.</li>
  <li><strong>Build the causation file.</strong> Government notifications, correspondence with sub-suppliers, transport records and the invocation notice should align in dates. Recovery suits over shock-period defaults are routinely decided on this paper trail.</li>
  <li><strong>Mind the commercial-suit timelines.</strong> Where the claim or defence arises in a commercial dispute of specified value, the Commercial Courts Act&rsquo;s regime — disclosure duties, the written statement deadline, costs for false denials — applies with full rigour to force majeure defences.</li>
  <li><strong>Address the money separately.</strong> Whether or not performance is excused, deal expressly with advances, bank guarantees and security deposits; excuse from performance and entitlement to retain money are separate questions.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> The best time to litigate force majeure is before signing. A clause that names the events a particular business actually fears, fixes a realistic causation threshold, and ties notice to a workable timeline converts a future catastrophe into a procedure. Parties reviewing standard-form supply, distribution or service agreements should treat the force majeure article with the same seriousness as price and term — in a crisis it is the only clause anyone reads.</p>
</div>
